/* data/learn-c/cs-12-01.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/cs-12-01.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["cs-12-01"] = {"1":{"s":"An operating system (OS) is system software that controls the computer hardware and runs the other programs. It sits between the user and the hardware, so the user need not know how the hardware works. Its goals are to make the computer easy to use and to use the CPU, memory and devices well. Examples: Windows, Linux, Android.","w":["The OS is software, a program. It is not hardware.","It manages resources (CPU, memory, files, devices); it is not an application like a word processor."],"x":["A computer has hardware (CPU, memory, disks, keyboard, screen) and many programs. If every program talked to the hardware by itself, they would clash. The operating system is the manager that stands in the middle.","The OS does three jobs. It gives the user an easy way to work (a desktop or a command line). It shares the CPU, memory and devices among the programs fairly. It protects one program and one user from another.","The computer system can be seen in layers: the hardware at the bottom, the operating system above it, the application programs above that, and the users on top. The OS is the layer that hides the hardware from everything above it."],"e":"When you click Print in a word processor, the program does not run the printer. It asks the OS; the OS uses the printer driver, queues the job and sends it to the printer."},"2":{"s":"An OS gives services to programs and users: it loads and runs programs, does input and output, manages files, lets processes communicate, finds and handles errors, allocates resources, protects and secures the system, and keeps accounts of resource use. A program asks for a service through a system call.","w":["A system call is the way a program asks the OS for a service. The OS does the work, not the program.","Learn the list of services in order; the list is a common board question."],"x":["Program execution: the OS loads a program into memory and runs it, and ends it when it finishes or fails.","Input and output operations: a program should not control a printer or disk directly, so the OS does it. File system: the OS creates, reads, writes and deletes files. Communication: processes in one computer or in different computers can exchange data through the OS.","Error detection: the OS notices errors in the CPU, memory or devices and takes an action. Resource allocation: when many users or programs run together, the OS decides who gets the CPU, memory and devices. Protection and security: it stops a user from reading the files of another. Accounting: it records which user used how much of the resources.","A system call is a request from a program to the OS to perform a service, for example to open a file. It is the door between the program and the kernel."],"e":"A game saves your score. The game makes a system call to create and write a file; the OS finds free space on the disk, writes the data and gives back a message of success."},"3":{"s":"Windows 98 is a single user, multitasking, graphical OS with plug and play and FAT32. Windows NT is a 32-bit, multiuser, multitasking, secure and portable OS with the NTFS file system and layers such as HAL, kernel and executive. Linux is a free, open source, multiuser, multitasking OS made of a kernel, a shell and utilities.","w":["Windows 98 is single user; Windows NT and Linux are multiuser.","Linux is open source: its program code is open to all. Windows is not."],"x":["Windows 98 was made for the home user. One person works at a time, but several programs can run together (single user, multitasking). It has a graphical interface, plug and play for new devices, long file names and the FAT32 file system for big disks, and it has the Internet built into the desktop.","Windows NT was made for servers and offices. It is a 32-bit OS that is multiuser and multitasking. It is secure (login, access rights, the NTFS file system), portable to different hardware, reliable, and it has networking built in. Its design is in layers: the hardware abstraction layer (HAL) hides the hardware, the kernel schedules the CPU, and the executive holds the managers (I/O, process, memory, security). Subsystems on top run programs for other systems.","Linux is a Unix-like OS started by Linus Torvalds. It is free and open source, multiuser and multitasking, and it runs on many kinds of hardware. Its main parts are the kernel (the core that controls memory, processes, files and devices), the shell (reads the commands of the user) and the utilities and applications. It has a hierarchical file system and it has several graphical desktops."],"e":"A small college lab with one user per PC could use Windows 98. A college server with many users logged in, each with private files, suits Windows NT or Linux."},"4":{"s":"Information management is the part of the OS that handles data kept on storage. It covers the file system (how files and directories are named, stored and found), device drivers (programs that control devices) and terminal input and output. The OS gives space to files by contiguous, linked or indexed allocation and keeps a list of free blocks, often as a bit map.","w":["Seek time is the movement of the head to the track; latency is the wait for the sector to come under the head. Do not mix them.","Internal fragmentation is waste inside an allocated block; external fragmentation is scattered free space between blocks."],"x":["A file is a named collection of related data on a disk. The file system decides how files are named, kept in directories (folders), given disk space and protected. A directory entry holds the file name, type, location, size, owner, protection and the dates.","The disk is divided into blocks, and a file takes a whole number of blocks. Allocation methods: contiguous (the blocks lie side by side; fast but it leaves gaps), linked (each block points to the next, so blocks can be anywhere) and indexed (an index block lists all the blocks of the file). The free blocks are tracked with a bit map, one bit for each block.","Access methods: sequential access reads the records one after another (like a tape), and direct access goes to any record at once (like a disk). Disk access time is the seek time (the head moves to the track) plus the latency time (the disk turns to the sector) plus the transfer time.","A device driver is a small program that knows how to control one device and translates the commands of the OS into commands the device understands. A terminal (keyboard and screen) is the input and output point of a user: dumb, smart or intelligent."],"e":"Saving a 10 KB file on a disk with 4 KB blocks takes 3 blocks. The last block is only partly used; this waste inside the block is internal fragmentation."},"5":{"s":"A process is a program in execution. Process management creates, schedules and ends processes. Multiprogramming keeps several programs in memory so the CPU never waits; multitasking and time sharing give each a short turn. A process moves through the states new, ready, running, waiting and terminated. Context switching saves one process and loads another. Priority decides who goes first.","w":["A program is passive (a file); a process is active (it is running).","Context switching is the change of the CPU from one process to another, with the state saved. It is not the same as multitasking."],"x":["A process has its own code, data, stack, registers and a state. The OS must create processes, schedule them on the CPU, let them wait for input and output, and end them.","Multiprogramming means keeping several programs in memory together. When one program waits for input and output, the CPU is given to another, so the CPU stays busy. The degree of multiprogramming is the number of processes in memory. Multitasking is running more than one task at a time by quick switching. Time sharing gives every user a short slice of CPU time (a quantum) in turn, so every user feels that the computer is his own.","Process states: a new process is being created; it becomes ready when it waits for the CPU; it runs when it has the CPU; it waits when it needs an input or output to finish; it is terminated when done. The scheduler picks the next ready process, using a rule such as priority. Priority is a number: a process of higher priority gets the CPU first.","Context switching: when the CPU changes from process A to process B, the OS saves the registers and program counter of A and loads those of B. Later A continues exactly where it stopped."],"e":"While a student prints a document (the printer is slow), the CPU runs the browser. When the printer is ready, the print process becomes ready again and gets a turn."},"6":{"s":"Memory management allocates memory to processes and takes it back, tracks which parts are free, and protects processes from each other. In a single user computer memory has the OS and one program. Partitioning divides memory into fixed or variable partitions. Paging cuts memory into equal frames and programs into pages; segmentation cuts a program into logical segments. Virtual memory lets a program larger than the memory run.","w":["Paging uses equal-size pages; segmentation uses unequal logical segments.","A page fault is not an error: the OS simply loads the missing page from the disk."],"x":["The memory map of a single user computer is simple: the operating system sits in one part (usually the low addresses), the user program in the next part, and the rest is free. Only one program is in memory at a time.","Partitioning is the division of memory so that many processes can be in memory. In fixed partitions the sizes are set at the start and do not change; a process in a larger partition wastes the unused space (internal fragmentation). In variable partitions a partition is made to the exact size of the process, so there is no internal waste, but free memory breaks into small pieces (external fragmentation). Compaction joins the pieces into one block.","Paging: memory is cut into equal frames, and a program into pages of the same size. Any page can go into any free frame, and a page table records where each page is. This removes external fragmentation. Segmentation: a program is cut into logical parts (code, data, stack) of different sizes, with a segment table for the addresses.","Virtual memory keeps only the needed parts of a program in memory and the rest on the disk. In demand paging a page is loaded only when it is needed. A page fault happens when the page asked for is not in memory; the OS loads it. A page that has been changed in memory is a dirty page and must be saved to the disk before it is replaced."],"e":"A process of 18 KB is placed in a fixed partition of 20 KB. The 2 KB that is left over cannot be used by any other process. That waste is internal fragmentation."},"7":{"s":"A GUI (graphical user interface) lets the user work with pictures: windows, icons, menus and a pointer moved by a mouse. A window is a rectangular area of the screen that shows one program. Basic features are the task list, drag, resize, minimize, maximize and close. A GUI is easier than typing commands.","w":["Minimize hides the window to the task bar; close ends the program. They are different.","GUI means graphical user interface. A command line interface is its opposite: text commands."],"x":["In a command line interface the user types commands and must remember them. A graphical user interface shows windows, icons and menus, and the user points and clicks. It is easier to learn and use, and it is used by Windows and by the Linux desktops.","A window is a rectangular area of the screen that shows one program or document. Many windows can be open together. The task list (task bar) shows the open programs and lets the user switch between them.","The basic operations on a window: drag (move it by its title bar), resize (change its size by its border), minimize (hide it to the task bar), maximize (make it fill the screen), restore (go back to the earlier size) and close (end it)."],"e":"To read two documents together, you open both, resize the windows to sit side by side, and drag the second window next to the first."},"8":{"s":"Security means protecting the system and its data from unauthorised use, damage and loss. Its elements are confidentiality, integrity, availability and authentication. Attacks are interruption, interception, modification and fabrication. Threats include computer worms (self-copying over a network) and viruses (attach to files). Prevent them with antivirus software, original software and backups.","w":["A virus needs a host file; a worm is a separate program that spreads by itself over a network.","Interruption attacks availability, interception attacks confidentiality, modification attacks integrity."],"x":["Security is the protection of the computer, its programs and its data from unauthorised access, damage and loss. The OS gives it by passwords, user accounts and access rights. The elements of security are confidentiality (only allowed persons can read), integrity (data is not changed without permission), availability (the system is ready for the right users) and authentication (the system checks who the user is).","Attacks on security: in interruption a resource is destroyed or blocked; in interception an unauthorised person gets the data; in modification the data is changed; in fabrication false data is put into the system.","A computer virus is a harmful program that attaches itself to another program or file and copies itself when that file runs. A computer worm is a harmful program that makes copies of itself and spreads over a network by itself, using up the network and memory. Viruses spread through infected disks, e-mail attachments and downloads.","Prevention: use original software from reliable sources, keep an antivirus program updated and scan every disk and download, do not open unknown e-mail attachments, and take regular backups. Removal: an antivirus program scans for the known patterns of viruses and removes them."],"e":"A friend gives you a pen drive. You scan it with the antivirus before opening any file. The scan finds a virus in one file and removes it, so your computer stays safe."}};
